[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Oh wow, UV printing got complicated quickly, didn\'t it? A year ago, Umake had pretty much this whole desktop UV printing thing to itself. Now Xool has turned up with three different machines, all called Omni. Heygears has decided UV printing should also somehow include'

[00:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'fullcolor 3D printing. And Morpho has arrived on Kickstarter, promising basically to outfeature everybody. So, [snorts] if you\'re trying to work out where to put a couple of grand or probably more right now, yeah, I can see why you\'re here. Because whether you\'re'

[00:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'buying your first one, adding another machine, trying to make money with it, chasing the best print quality, or you\'ve already narrowed this down to two, and just need somebody to break the tie, the whole point of this video is to satisfy you in a way that you only'

[00:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'experienced before you got married. And I\'ve used all of them. More importantly, I\'ve deliberately been doing the same tests on them so we can stop comparing the BS marketing pages and actually compare the bloody printers. Two of'

[00:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'these products you can buy today. Two are still pre-release Kickstarter machines where you\'re not just buying the hardware, you\'re also buying into a dream with a little bit of faith. And that matters because there\'s a big'

[01:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'difference between this machine can do this and [music] this is actually the machine you should buy to do it. But by the end of this video, I hope you at least know where your money\'s going. [music] At worst, I\'m confident you\'ll have it down to'

[01:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'two. And I\'ve already got individual videos on all four to help you finish that argument. So, hi, I\'m Ross and this is Fire Hammer Videos. And if the audio sounds a bit weird today, it\'s only because, well, as you can see, I\'m not'

[01:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'in my studio. I\'m actually on my family holiday and it took me right up to the last minute to get this script right. So, please, I hope you watch it. Let\'s move on. So, first thing, these aren\'t four equivalent machines, which actually makes this a bit easier because if'

[01:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you\'re desperate to get one now, there\'s only actually two available. Euthake is the known quantity here. The E1 has been out for a year. Lots of people own one. The software has matured, the accessories exist, and if something goes'

[01:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'wrong, there\'s a decent chance somebody else has already done exactly the same stupid thing and posted the answer online. It is the established option here, and that has real value in simply knowing what ownership looks like.'

[02:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Whereas X tool is also a pretty finished product. Although apparently and amusingly one Omni wasn\'t enough. So there are three of them and they mirror a lot of already worked with Yuimake. But they give you more space, more'

[02:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'height, potentially more speed depending on which one you buy and the benefit of plugging into XTOL\'s existing ecosystem. And based on what we\'ve seen from them over the years, I\'ve got more confidence in Xool than anyone else here to keep'

[02:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'pushing the software forward whenever they or somebody else comes up with a good idea. Then we get on to Heyge Gears and Morpho and this is where the comparison changes slightly because at the time of recording, both are still on'

[02:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Kickstarter and the machines I\'ve used are prototypes. So, we can\'t fairly compare final hardware quality or long-term reliability against two machines that are already sitting in people\'s homes and businesses. But, we'

[02:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'do still know enough to compare what they\'re offering, how those features properly work together, and what they\'re actually going to give you in terms of real product value. I\'ve used them, printed with them, followed the workflows, tested the features, and'

[03:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'broken things of them. Sometimes deliberately, more accurately, never deliberately. But that gives us something the spec sheet never will. Because a spec sheet is very good at telling us what a machine has in a way'

[03:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that\'s dressed up to sound good, often with bigger numbers, it\'s much less useful at telling us whether any of these things actually matter. So hey is probably the easiest one to understand. They\'ve gone heavily after print quality'

[03:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'with the G1X getting the faster i3200 print head, light cyan and light magenta for professional photo level color with super smooth gradients and skin tones. UV curing in both directions and then'

[03:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'obviously fullcolor 3D printing which nobody else is officially offering. So if fullcolor 3D printing is literally the only reason you click this video, get that one. Thanks for watching. See'

[04:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you next time. Please use my affiliate links and check the member scroll at the end. For everyone else, we should probably continue. Now Moro is coming at this from a very different direction. bigger enclosed printing, a useful screen on the machine, custom color'

[04:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'profiles, repeatable jig workflows, and a whole setup that feels like somebody started by asking, "How do we make one of these useful when you need to produce 50 of the same thing?" Then it appears the marketing team went and looked at'

[04:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'everything else that everyone else was offering, panicked, and went, "Oh, yeah, yeah, Morpho can do that, too." Just with a fairly important but invisible asterisk saying, "Just not officially." We\'ll come back to that and a few other'

[04:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'bits that raise more questions for me than answers. But before we go any further, remember that Heyge Gears and Morpho are still prototypes. Things will change, things should improve, some things may disappear, some things may be'

[04:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'added. And Kickstarter being Kickstarter, if either of these arrives 3 months late or more, nobody should be particularly shocked. You aren\'t buying a machine off the shelf. You\'re backing what these machines are intended to become at launch. But those are the'

[05:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'reputations going in. Euth is the established one. X Tool is the allrounder. Hey Gears is chasing print quality in fullcolor 3D. Morpho is chasing production. So with that in mind, let\'s start picking these things'

[05:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'apart. And we\'ll start with something incredibly shallow. Which one looks nicest? Now completely subjective. People will argue it\'s unimportant yet still something we\'re all going to subconsciously judge them on anyway'

[05:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'because first impressions do matter just like the names. And yes, both Yui Make and Morpho both sound a bit dafted. Now Yuth still looks good, but next to the others now, it feels a bit more toy-ike. Not bad, just softer, rounder, and less'

[05:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'serious looking than some of the newer machines. Xool is probably the best looking of the four to me. Solid construction, cleaner lines. It just has that more premium product design where somebody has clearly spent time thinking'

[05:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'about how the whole thing should look, not just how it works. Hey Gears is much simpler. Everything is black. Everything is fairly plain and whilst there\'s nothing really wrong with it, it\'s also probably the least visually interesting'

[06:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of the four. [music] It looks more like a piece of equipment than something designed to show off. Morpho sits somewhere between Yui Make and Xool. The color scheme gives it a more professional look, but the rounded edges'

[06:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'soften that a bit, and some of the hatches and overall assembly make it feel slightly less premium than the X tool. So, for [music] me, Xool wins on looks, followed by Morpho, Euthi Make, and then Heygears. So, one category down'

[06:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and absolutely nothing useful has happened yet. From here, I\'m going to keep this in a very deliberate order so it stays easy to follow. If you\'re new to UV printing, some bits might not make sense the first time I mention them, and'

[06:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that\'s fine. I haven\'t skipped anything. We just haven\'t gotten there yet. Stick with the parts you do understand, and the rest will make sense when we reach them. So, ports and other openings. This is mostly going to matter when you\'re'

[06:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'deciding where these things can physically live. So, Yui Make, hey Gears, and Morpho all use an external power supply with the DC import low down on the left side. Um, and Morpho have illuminated power buttons, whilst Hey'

[07:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Gears has a normal rocker switch. X tool is much neater here with the power supply built into the machine and a normal AC cable going straight into the back and the power button is on the front which lights up all rainbow like.'

[07:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'All four have Wi-Fi builtin, but Yui Make and Morpho also give us Ethernet, which is definitely preferable if these are going into a more permanent business or fleet setup. An X tool also has another connection on the back, which is'

[07:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a proper exhaust port. Next is the panels, and this is where the dimensions of the machine stop being useful because if you need regular access down one or both sides, you need to leave room for that, too. Euthimate needs access on the'

[07:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'right side for removing its clip-in panel and that\'s where the cleaning cartridge contains both the cleaning fluid and waste in one unit. The air filter is accessed from the front behind a magnetic panel. So the left side here'

[07:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'stays clear. X tool needs both sides. The air filter cartridge is behind a nicely magnetized panel on the left while the right side similar panel houses the individual cleaning agents and a separate waste container. Hey,'

[08:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Ggears are similar with the filter on the left and a simple waste bottle on the right that you can remove and empty. And these prototype panels are a bit more clip in rather than magnets. For cleaning fluid here, that goes in at the'

[08:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'top, but we\'ll come back to that later. Morpho also needs both sides, but it doesn\'t use or have a place for cleaning fluids. [music] Ink goes in behind the partially magnetized panel on the left, while the air filter and waste bottle'

[08:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'sit together on the right. So the value here is if you\'re trying to do something like squeeze as many of these as possible onto racking, Yuimate does have a small advantage in only really demanding regular access down one side. Now for those of you out there still'

[08:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'obsessed with the claim that size matters or to be more mature than me and just for general context, dimensions in millime. Um is 590 wide by 250 deep and 407 high. Xool is 714x374x469.'

[09:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Heygears is 580x 660x 510 and Moro is 660x430x 390. [snorts] So in simple terms, X tool'

[09:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is the widest. Hegears is by far the deepest [music] and tallest. Morpho is actually the shortest and Euimate takes up the least space overall because it\'s smaller. Weight wise, and this is based on the advertised specs, Yui makes'

[09:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'around 20 kilos, Morpho\'s around 32, Xual\'s just over 40, and Hers is currently publishing conflicting figures between 25 and 45 kilos. Now, having lifted the thing, 45 certainly feels'

[09:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'more believable once you account for that massive metal build plate. But I\'m not calling that definitive until they sort their spec sheet out. Then there\'s the doors. Yuthake X tool and Heygearss all have large front and rear doors that'

[09:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'fold down and can be easily removed. Euthole use simple clips or magnets. The Hey Gears prototype still uses screws to remove the doors, although the brand have already said they\'re changing that. Morpho is slightly different. The front'

[10:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'door flips down and comes off normally, but the rear is a much smaller clip-in flap. So once you remove it, it takes up less space than a door when you store it. Small detail, but sensible. The other thing the three newer machines'

[10:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'improve on is visibility and maintenance because X tool, Hers, and Morpho now have much larger viewing windows than Yuiake. Now, that sounds cosmetic until you\'re actually using one. Being able to properly see the print in progress is'

[10:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'obviously useful, but those larger openings also make it much easier to get your head, hands, and tools into the machine when you\'re cleaning, checking something, or inevitably trying to work out what the hell just happened. Yuim still works fine, but next to the other'

[10:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'three, it feels noticeably more enclosed when you\'re actually trying to get at something inside. Small thing, but genuinely nice in use. And finally, the obvious external extra. Morpho has a flip-up screen on top. And unlike the'

[10:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'others, you can actually do quite a lot from the machine itself. Setup, firmware updates, maintenance tasks, and even starting jobs you use regularly can all be done on there. whilst the other machines lean much more heavily on their'

[11:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'computer software and mobile apps. And that starts to give Moro another small advantage because if you\'re doing repeated production work, every trip you don\'t have to make between your printer and computer is time saved. Now, inks are where things get weird and start to'

[11:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'get complicated. Again, stick with it. For now, we\'re just going to look at how they physically go into each machine and the different systems they use. Then later, like with the cleaning fluids and other stuff we\'ve just glossed over, we\'ll come back and explain how all of'

[11:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this actually works together. [snorts] So, Yuimake is probably the most familiar. All the cartridges plug straight in the back with a press to lock and release mechanism. Push them in, they click into place. Push again, they clack back out. And they\'re in the sensible order of cyan, magenta, yellow,'

[11:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and key, which is black, giving us CMYK, followed then by white and gloss. White is available in both rigid and flexible versions. Relatively simple. X Tool works much in the same way with'

[11:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'cartridges loading into the back. But this is where having three different machines all called Omni starts becoming especially unhelpful. The basic Omni uses the same CMK white and varnish setup as Yuimake, just in a different'

[12:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'order. And at the time of recording, there\'s no flexible white option for the initial bank of six inks. And also, I can\'t be the only one whose OCD flares up when brands take the common CMK and decide the order of inks would be better'

[12:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'in some different layout because that\'s both X tool and Morpho. Now, the Dual UV1 adds a second bank, a second print head. But that second bank of inks also now stores a larger rigid white. And now'

[12:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you can get flexible white in a larger cartridge too, along with fluorescent red and yellow, which is magenta, not red anyway. And then we\'ve got the UV plusDT version of the 01. And in this case, it uses that second bank for'

[12:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'separate fabric inks instead. Completely different to UV inks. And no, you can\'t go from dual UV to DT or vice versa. But again, the DT inks come in CMK, this time with a larger white. But all three are the same basic 01 machine, just a'

[13:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'very different ink setup depending on which Omni you actually buy. My review covers that in more detail. Hey Gears, though, is completely different. There are no cartridges. Instead, you get bottles of ink with screw on nozzles'

[13:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'with individual wells on the back of the machine, and you physically pour in the ink until the light turns green. Now, for normal UV printing, you\'ve got CMYK, white, and varnish. Plus, this time, light cyan, and light magenta, which is'

[13:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'typically used by Pro2D printers for more accurate gradients and skin tones. Cleaning fluid is also filled through this same system, too. And yes, technically with this being an open'

[13:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'container, you could pour another brand\'s ink in here. But whether that\'s sensible when cheap ink could potentially clog an expensive print head is another matter. That\'s your call. But then if you want to use the G1X for full'

[13:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'color 3D printing, the whole rear ink system changes. The wells, lines, and parts of the head assembly are replaced, and you have to add a large external refill station, too. Now, in this mode,'

[14:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you\'ve still got CMYK and gloss, but the light cyan and magenta channels now become an extra white and the soluble support material. So, yeah, be aware switching between 2D and 3D printing on this machine is definitely more involved'

[14:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'than pressing a button. Takes about 5 minutes, but it\'s still a pain in the ass. [music] Now, Morpho takes us back to cartridges again. Here they load in the left side behind the panel we looked at earlier with the usual CMYK, white,'

[14:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and varnish. The colors use smaller cartridges whilst the white and varnish use larger ones. And white again here comes in rigid or flexible versions. [music] So UI is the straightforward cartridge system. X tool starts'

[14:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'similarly but changes quite dramatically depending on which Omni you buy. Hey has refillable wells, Pro Colors and a completely different material setup for 3D. And Morpho brings us back to cartridges just loaded in through the'

[14:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'side. Now, before we get any deeper into how these things work, let\'s stick with physical capabilities. Specifically, how much stuff can you actually fit in them? Now, they all basically have two working areas. A smaller bed you can use with'

[15:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the machine closed, and a much larger bed that moves backwards and forwards through the machine with the doors open. Um has a 330x 90 mm small bed and a 330x420 mm large bed with a maximum'

[15:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'object height of 60 mm on both. X tool only increases the small bed slightly to 330x 122 mm with the same 330x 420 mm large bed, but now the maximum object'

[15:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'height jumps massively to 150 mm. Hey goes slightly bigger again with 330x 130 for the small bed and 150 mm object height. The large bed again is that 330x'

[15:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '420, but maximum height here drops slightly to 130 mil. And based on their advertising, when you print in 3D mode, you can use those full volumes for the entire prints. Then morpho, the small'

[16:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'bed is the wow here because that jumps all the way to 330x 220 with a maximum object height of 168 mm. But the large bed is once again 330x420,'

[16:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this time with the maximum height dropping to 133. So the large print area is basically the same across all four. The big differences are the enclosed space and object height. Euthimake is easily the most restrictive at 60 mm'

[16:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'tall. X tool and HGAS get you to around 150. And Morpho gives you the largest enclosed area and the tallest object capacity. And it manages this because unlike the others, Morpho can also move'

[16:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the print head backwards and forwards along the Yaxis rather than relying entirely on the bed to move the object underneath, which is clever, but it does add another moving system that needs to'

[16:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'stay accurate over time. Now, I have seen some horizontal lines on a few prototype prints, although later prints didn\'t show the same issues. So, I can\'t say whether that was the gantry, prototype bugs, or something completely'

[17:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'unrelated. But the gantry itself is something I\'ll be watching because we simply don\'t have enough finished machines in enough people\'s hands yet to know how accurately that extra movement holds up after months of real use. So,'

[17:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'now we know how much area these machines can cover, or for the sake of 3D, the volume it prints to. How quickly can they actually cover it? Well, thankfully, despite all the different machines, this bit comes down to'

[17:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'basically two Epson print heads. The [snorts] F1080 and the i3200. And the useful difference is actually quite simple. The F1080 has 1,080 nozzles and the i3200 has 3,200. So, the i3200 can throw out a lot more'

[17:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'ink in each pass, giving it much more potential speed potential. Because that [snorts] doesn\'t mean every i3200 machine automatically prints three times faster because resolution, layers, ink,'

[17:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and how each brand actually uses the head all affect that. To me, a lot of this is going to be the same as FDM printing. Brands can push to maximum hardware speed, but often with the impact of some degradation in print'

[18:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'quality. But still, generally, the i3200 in raw hardware terms means faster. Knowing that, let\'s break them down. Umake uses the F1080. X Tool also uses the F1080 on the standard Omni, whilst'

[18:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the dual UV and UV plusDT versions use two F1080s. What that second head is doing depends on which Omni you bought as we just covered with the inks. It\'s either being used to throw out more white per pass or it\'s entirely separate'

[18:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and dedicated to apparel printing only. Now Heygears gives us both options depending on the machine. The standard G1 uses the F1080, whilst the G1X, which is the only one that can do 3D prints,'

[18:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'steps up to the i3200. But Heygears also does something unique in the curing because it\'s actually got UV lamps on both sides of the head unit. So when it prints moving left or right, it can also cure the ink in both'

[18:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'directions. Everyone else can print in both directions, too. But there, the UV curing is only coming from one side. We\'ll get on to that when we look at print quality. Now, Morpho also comes in two versions. The standard machine quite'

[19:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'obviously will use the F1080, whilst the Pro gets the i3200. And Morpho\'s FAQ says the F1080 version can actually be upgraded to the i3200 later, too. So, the simple version make F1080. Xool is'

[19:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'one or two F1080s. Hey, Gears gives you the F1080 for the G1 or the i3200 on the G1X. And Morpho gives you the same basic choice between F1080 and i3200 with an upgrade path. So now we know what ink'

[19:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'they use and what\'s firing it out. We need to talk about keeping all of this working because UV printers aren\'t machines that you switch off and forget about a month. You do need to use them or lose them. And this needs to become'

[19:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'very common knowledge. Ink can settle, lines can block, and print heads can clog. So all four machines need to regularly maintain themselves even when you\'re not actively printing. And even when they are properly maintained, you'

[20:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'need to be aware that these print heads are classed as consumable parts. So when they wear out, which they do, you will be expected to replace them. Now, Euthate keeps things relatively simple with that cartridge we saw earlier. It'

[20:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'contains the cleaning fluid and it collects the waste in the same unit, so both are dealt with together. X tool separates everything out. The cleaning agents each have their own bottles with a completely separate waste container.'

[20:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So when something runs out or fills up, you\'re only dealing with that individual part. Hey, Gears is simpler again. The cleaning fluid gets poured into the machine through the same rear tank as the inks, whilst waste collects in the'

[20:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'bottle on the side, which you have to remove and empty yourself. Then Morpho does something completely different. There is no everyday cleaning fluid at all. Instead, it uses a mechanical cleaning reel to wipe the print head.'

[20:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And interestingly, it wipes front to back as everyone should rather than across the different colors from side to side like the others with rubber wipers. And that should help reduce inks being dragged between neighboring nozzles and'

[21:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'potentially affecting the start of the next print. And removing cleaning fluid entirely is a pretty significant advantage. And we\'re going to put an actual value on that when we get to running costs. But whichever one you buy, these all still need to stay'

[21:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'powered on. So they can periodically refresh the lines and the print head. That means small amount of inks are going to get purged even when you\'re not printing. And on Yuthi Makeake X tool and HIA\'s cleaning fluid, too. So you'

[21:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'really can\'t just buy one of these, use it twice, switch it off, and put it in a cupboard for 6 weeks. And just to put more in Morpho\'s corner, they\'ve also discussed a separate flush material that would actually let you purge the lines'

[21:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'from where the ink goes in all the way through to the tool head itself for proper long-term storage, which is a useful answer to that problem. But that\'s different from the normal day-to-day cleaning system. So, to'

[21:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'recap, as always, day-to-day UI combines everything into one cartridge. Xool separates it out. Hey, Ggears has you fill the cleaner and empty the waste yourself. And Morpho removes the cleaning fluid from normal maintenance'

[22:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'entirely. Which now brings us neatly on to the other thing. Those filters and that exhaust port were there for what happens to the air while all this stuff is printing because UV ink can give off VOCC\'s and other stuff you ideally don\'t'

[22:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'want to spend all day breathing. And regardless of what any of these brands say about low odor or various certifications, if you can vent a UV printer\'s air outside, do it now. You make as standard has a carbon filter'

[22:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'behind that front panel, but the air appears to pass through it once before being pushed into the face plate and then back out into your environment. They\'ve now released the P1 air filter and an exhaust kit. Either of which replaces that internal filter and gives'

[22:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you either additional larger one pass filtration or much better a direct route outside. Now, Xool has thought about this from the start. It has a proper exhaust connection built into the back'

[23:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'with a hose included in the box. So you can throw that out of a window or connect it to their existing desktop purifier or the much larger AP2 [music] which also works as a pre filter so you can vent it outside afterwards. Now even'

[23:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'without this it also recirculates the air internally through the carbon filter whilst the machine\'s enclosed. Now Hers does the same basic thing. So does Morpho. Again, rather than one pass through the filter and out into your'

[23:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'room, the enclosed air keeps getting circulated through the carbon again and again. More passes means more opportunities to filter some of these potentially nasty things out. And this is where that much larger second'

[23:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'enclosed area on Morpho becomes especially useful because more printing can happen with the chamber closed. But Morpho are pushing the whole low odor and green guard angle quite hard and I start getting a little uncomfortable'

[23:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'with how this could be interpreted. Yes, it may smell less. It may produce fewer VOCC\'s, but what VOCC\'s is it filtering out the not so bad ones and leaving the really bad ones or the other way round?'

[24:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'There\'s still a very clear hazardous to lung symbol on the side of the ink carts. And it seems that a lot of these green guard certifications may only be relevant for when the materials are cured. I\'ve said may on that because I\'m'

[24:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'not clear, but I just think they\'re pushing safe really hard when it isn\'t. I\'d still treat it exactly like you would any other UV printer here and vent it if you can. Now, there\'s more to talk about on their ink and some of their'

[24:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'claims, but we\'ll save the rest of that conversation for later. In summary, Xtool has the best answer here because getting air out of the room entirely is better than repeatedly trying to clean it. And the biggest chunk of that solution comes in the box with upgrade'

[24:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'options available. The others require add-on accessories to do that. Umate comes in second because they now have exhaust solutions that at least exist. And whichever one of those you buy, I\'d still say if you can exhaust it outside,'

[25:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I [music] would. If Heygears and Moro were to add extraction, it\'s going to need to be with a similar compression fit add-on module that they release in the future. So, that covers the hardware, but every single print starts'

[25:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'in the software. And this, to me, is where a brilliant machine can very quickly become annoying to actually own or vice versa. The interface between your having the product, I\'ve argued on'

[25:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this channel for a while, is more important in some ways than the hardware itself. In any industry, different brands can chuck essentially the same hardware in a box. But the easier they make it for you to make the thing you'

[25:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'want as fast as you possibly can is a huge point about all of these machines. So, let\'s go over it. Um basically set the bar that everyone else needed to meet. Connect the printer. It knows'

[25:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'which beds installed, drop some material on it, take a photo, line everything up, pick your material settings, and click print. Clean, simple, very little thinking required. and it\'s had a year of updates now, so the whole workflow'

[26:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'feels properly settled. Now, Xtool came in and pretty much matched that from day one. Drop the artwork in, set up the job, position it, and print. [snorts] But because Xtool Studio already works across their other machines, you also'

[26:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'get the benefit of everything living in one ecosystem. So, you can actually print something here and then move that same project across to one of their lasers and cut it out afterwards. That\'s genuinely useful and it\'s where the'

[26:26] [scene:talk]
