AUDIO: 'argument in favor of these more proprietary softwares starts to make sense. Heygears is where things currently get more awkward. Their software still feels much more like it\'s been built around their existing 3D'

[26:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'printing workflow rather than have many of the benefits the other brands have with 2D. Every job here currently wants you to pick the printer, the add-ons, the application, the material profile, then finally start setting up the actual'

[26:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'job in a similar way to the others. Now, none of that\'s impossible, and it doesn\'t make the machine unusable. There\'s just far more steps here than there really needs to be. And unfortunately for Hagars, software has historically been one of their weaker'

[27:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'areas. Their online support guides are pretty much non-existent. Basic menu ordering on 3D printers has been left messy for nearly 3 years, and the profiles on their cure stations are laughably inconsistent. They still fully work, but the delivery lacks a'

[27:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'significant amount of polish. Now, Morpho went hard in almost completely the other way from everyone else. The software I tested was so focused on professional production workflows that they seemed to have completely forgotten'

[27:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'about the normal user. I was clicking through jig sizes and applications before I could even see a preview window. And even then, I was dancing between multiple design spaces and a workspace just to get something ready to print. Now, for somebody who just wants'

[27:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to drag one thing in, line it up, and click print like every other machine, this was frustrating to the point of a waste of time. Now, thankfully, they\'ve already fixed that. The normal workflow is now much simpler. But importantly,'

[27:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'all of that heavier production workflow is still there. And for batch printing, that part is actually brilliant. You can create artwork frames aligned to a planned community of jigs in their open'

[28:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'jig system. So once everything is set up once, repeat jobs don\'t even need another photo or manual alignment. Put the products in the jig, load the artwork into the frames, and click print. So ironically, the workflow that'

[28:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'initially made more the most complicated for normal jobs also make it the simplest of all four when you\'re repeatedly making the same thing. Which brings us neatly onto alignment. Because getting the artwork into the software is'

[28:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'one thing. Getting it onto the object in the right place is another. Now, Yuimake has a top- down camera. Take a photo, position the artwork over the object, and click print. It works okay, but cameras do have an obvious limitation.'

[28:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'They\'re not the most accurate with objects directly underneath them. Move an object towards the edge of a print plate, and it starts seeing more of the sides of these objects rather than looking straight down at the tops, which can make precise positioning even'

[29:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'harder. Now, Xool gets around that completely differently. Instead of relying on a camera, it\'s got a line scanner built into the print head, which scans across the actual bed, basically using the same principle as a normal'

[29:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'paper scanner. Now, in my testing, this was incredibly accurate across the whole print area. Even objects towards the edges lined up almost exactly where the software said they would. Now, Hegears currently uses a camerabased system on'

[29:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the prototype I tested, but they have already announced they\'re going to be adding a line scanner, too. So assuming that makes it to the finished machine, they\'ll get that same advantage. The Morpho though seems to get much more'

[29:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'complicated because it uses two cameras along with an infrared height measuring system. And they\'re also developing a camera in the print head for more accurate top- down positioning where the print head can actually move over'

[29:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'different objects, take multiple photos, and stitch them together. The problem is, so far all that extra hardware hasn\'t given me better results than Xtools much simpler line scanner. Objects are still distorted towards the'

[30:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'edges of camera views. I\'ve had more misaligned prints here than on any other machines, and the system has also misread object heights. Now, this may all improve before launch, but right now, Xtools line scanner has given me'

[30:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the best alignment results of all four. And hey, Gears appears to be heading towards the same solution. And with Morpho though, its jig system makes a lot of this argument quite moot if you\'re looking at this specifically for'

[30:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'batch workflows because there you\'re not photographing and aligning every individual object anyway. But for someone doing lots of varied jobs though, right now even just in concept, the more complex camera system seems'

[30:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'only a little more useful than Yuthi makes single camera and [music] still not as helpful as a line scanner. Now, I\'m more than happy to be proven wrong, but I mean proven, not just shouted out in the comments because someone really'

[30:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'wants to believe. I\'m not saying it\'s a bad machine, guys. I\'m about to recommend it to an absolute ton of people. Just stick with me. I\'m just saying this practically isn\'t as useful. So, can can we chill out? I\'m I\'m not trying to be unfair. Okay. Print'

[31:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'quality. Now, thankfully, I planned ahead for once and printed the same test images on all four machines specifically to do these types of comparisons. And from a normal viewing distance, [music] honestly guys, most of them look pretty'

[31:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'similar. And the difference for the vast majority of people here is going to be between, "Oh yeah, yeah, I can I can see it now that you explain it to me," rather than, "Oh my god, that\'s absolutely amazing and so much better."'

[31:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And for all of these, I printed these onto the same black A4 sheet of printed PLA plastic with three layers of white underneath the color so we can actually compare them like for like. So, starting with Yuimake, this Northlight images'

[31:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'test chart came out really nicely. The focal points are gradient, they\'re smooth, the colors are consistent, and especially areas like the blue sky around the church and the gray frames around all of the images stay pretty'

[31:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'clean even when you get in close. However, the blacks were sometimes much more pronounced than I expected. Clearly, like those areas have been given extra height with additional layers of black ink. Also, the overall'

[32:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'surface finish has a soft sheen to it. Now, X tool is very similar, but when you get into those same transition areas, they\'re a little grainier. You can just about see it with the naked eye, but under macro, it does become'

[32:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'more obvious. Those smooth transitions start showing more of the individual dots of ink being used to create the colors. The blacks are still slightly raised, just not as much. And again, we\'ve got that same soft sheen across'

[32:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the surface. Then, we\'ve got hgearss. And for me, the G1X was easily the best print quality of the four. The gradients are incredibly smooth because instead of getting light blue by seeing fewer blue'

[32:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'dots on a white background, those areas actually look more like lighter blue, which may well be helped by the light cyan ink we talked about earlier. Similarly, the skin tones look much cleaner, which likely comes from both of'

[33:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the light inks, cyan and magenta. Hegars is clearly the best option here for those prioritizing replication of near professional photograde quality on objects under close interrogation. And'

[33:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the surface finish is noticeably more matte than any of the others. And I think that matters because all of these machines can add gloss varnish on top if you want something shinier. But you can\'t go the other way with any of them.'

[33:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So starting more matte gives us more range across the final finish textures available. And this is an assumption, but I think maybe the dual UV lamps may be helping here by curing in both directions and giving the ink less time'

[33:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to move or mix before it\'s locked into place. Then we\'ve got Morpho. And unfortunately, this was very clearly the weakest print quality of all four. And I asked Morpho to print the same test'

[33:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'themselves, too. And we were both seeing the same kinds of results, which indicates to me this isn\'t limited to the unit I tested. Here, the surface finish came out the glossiest. And [music] when you look closely at the'

[34:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'gradients, we can clearly see the individual colored dots being used to build them. And this was at the highest resolution. Pushing the tests even further, I printed some color charts on varying levels of white ink. And it was'

[34:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the darker colors from orange through to warm greens where it stood out the most for me. The test squares started getting muddier and less defined with clear lines the darker they became. Almost like the inks are blending together'

[34:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'before they cure. And yeah, look, I know I\'m spending more time covering the worst result here than I did praising the good ones, but that\'s because I rarely need to defend a test when something comes out looking great. But I'

[34:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'think this is where one distinction matters because Morpho talk a lot about color accuracy, and they often talk about how small text they can print as though the other machines aren\'t capable of the exact same thing when they\'re using comparable hardware. But the thing'

[34:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is, color accuracy and print quality aren\'t the same thing. You can reproduce the correct color and still have visible grain, blending, ghosting, or a surface finish you don\'t like or don\'t want.'

[35:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '[snorts] And in fairness, Morpho are still working on this. They keep reminding me it\'s a prototype. So are other brands, but fair enough. They might just be further along in this specific area. And I do fully expect it'

[35:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to improve. I just can\'t tell you by how much yet. So, if we base this section purely on the prints I\'ve got in front of me today, Hey Gears is first, you second, X Tool third, although honestly'

[35:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'those two are pretty close, and Moro is currently fourth with more to talk about on this subject shortly. Again though, as I said in the beginning of this section, from a normal viewing distance, they all look about the same. So, I'

[35:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'wouldn\'t choose between these four based on print quality alone. But Morpho is the one where I do think we need to look a little harder at what we\'re actually being promised. Because with any Kickstarter, same with Heygears, don\'t'

[35:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'just look at what they\'re telling you, ask what they\'re not. In fact, be vigilant, avoid a shock, and question everything. And for me, it\'s things like this. Morpho says they\'ve got an open'

[36:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'ink system. That\'s great. But they also say only their ink is the reason you don\'t need cleaning fluid and why the print head should last longer. And I just want to emphasize should and we also don\'t know how much longer in'

[36:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'equivalent usage tests. But the question I\'ve got is okay so I can use third party ink but don\'t I now need cleaner? Well if so where does that go? Because I can\'t see a feed line for it anywhere on the machine. And then the same with the'

[36:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'fluorescent inks and 3D printing. They are technically possible. Yeah. But once we\'re replacing ink channels, purging lines and sourcing materials Moro themselves don\'t fully support. Whilst they do advertise them as capabilities,'

[36:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that\'s very different from another brand actually having those features built around supported materials and existing workflows. And because Morpho are also making such a big deal about the'

[36:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'properties of their ink, yeah, naturally, I\'m going to ask if there\'s a trade-off. Now, maybe there isn\'t. Brilliant if that\'s true. I\'ve just seen enough eco- focus 3D printing materials make similar promises, yet they quietly'

[37:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'sacrifice something elsewhere. That\'s why I want these claims proven. Because if it really is as simple as making the ink less corrosive, reducing emissions, and removing cleaning fluid without'

[37:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
